CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a single-row block diagram of one convolutional layer, built from two perspective slab tensors with a labeled box between them. At the left a thin white slab is drawn as three stacked sheets seen nearly edge-on; the number 3 sits beneath it, 128 annotates its vertical left edge, 128 annotates the receding top edge, and the header above reads x_in in R, of shape 3 x 128 x 128. A hollow outlined block arrow points rightward into a rounded rectangle whose two lines of text read Filter Bank with 3x3 filters. A second hollow arrow leads to the right-hand slab, drawn in the same style but far thicker, with sets of three dots among its sheets marking omitted layers; 96 labels its depth, 128 its right vertical edge and 128 its lower receding edge, and the header reads x_out in R, of shape 96 x 128 x 128. Rendering both tensors identically reduces the comparison to thickness alone: the spatial dimensions stay at 128 by 128 while the channel count grows from 3 to 96. The layout supports working out, with concrete numbers, how many parameters each filter holds and how many filters the bank contains.

PROJECTION TYPE: oblique
Call setCameraView({ projection: 'oblique', obliqueAngleDeg: 45, depthScale: 0.5, heightFraction }) after all geometry is added — a standard cabinet-oblique pose; the plan does not measure per-figure oblique angles. The scaffold implements oblique as a shear of the eye-space projection matrix, so the front face stays undistorted, depth edges leave at obliqueAngleDeg, and labels track the shear automatically. Build the TRUE 3D object — the shear is the camera's job. Do NOT shear geometry, do NOT wrap the figure in a sheared parent Object3D, and do NOT disable automatic matrix updates. The scaffold also provides the reveal interaction: dragging eases the shear out to expose the real 3D shape and a toggle button returns to the printed view — do not implement either yourself.
AXIS ASSIGNMENT: the pose is pinned, so in BoxGeometry(w, h, d) the w axis is screen-right, h is screen-up, and d is the receding diagonal. Before writing each BoxGeometry, look at the source image and decide which of the object's dimensions actually recedes there — do not assume it is the one the domain calls "depth". In feature-map / layer-stack diagrams the channel count is usually the w (horizontal) extent along the flow direction and a spatial dimension is d. Sanity-check the silhouette against the source: a block that should read as a long bar pointing at its neighbours must be long in w, not in d, or it will render as a sliver shooting into the page. If GEOMETRY NOTES assigns the axes in a way that contradicts the source image's silhouette, follow the image.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Input tensor slab x_in drawn as 3 identical thin sheets stacked along a depth diagonal receding up-and-right, front-left face a true 128x128 square
  - Thin vertical separating lines on the input slab's top receding face marking the 3 channel sheets
  - Label '3' centered beneath the input slab (channel count)
  - Label '128' along the input slab's left vertical edge (height)
  - Label '128' along the input slab's upper receding edge (width/depth of the image plane)
  - Header text above the input slab: x_in ∈ R^{3×128×128}
  - Hollow outlined block arrow (pentagon-shaped, white fill, black outline) pointing right from input slab to filter box
  - Rounded rectangle box with thick black outline and white fill, centered between the arrows
  - Two-line text inside the box: 'Filter Bank with' / '3x3 filters'
  - Second identical hollow outlined block arrow pointing right from the box to the output slab
  - Output tensor slab x_out drawn in the same style, far thicker, with many parallel sheet-separating lines
  - Two sets of three dots ('...') among the output slab sheets — one on the upper receding face, one on the front face — marking omitted channels
  - Label '96' centered beneath the output slab (channel count)
  - Label '128' along the output slab's right vertical edge
  - Label '128' along the output slab's lower receding edge
  - Header text above the output slab: x_out ∈ R^{96×128×128}
  - Hidden-until-clicked overlay inside the filter box: a 3×3×3 stack of small weight cells representing one selected filter
  - Highlight outline linking the selected filter's three 3×3 kernel slices to the three input channel sheets and to one output sheet

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Oblique layout, all elements on one horizontal row centered at y=0; +X screen-right, +Y screen-up, +Z the receding diagonal (drawn up-and-right at ~40° on screen). INPUT SLAB: front-left face is a true square 4.0 (X-height? no) — precisely: face lies in the X–Y plane as 0-thickness? Use box X = depth(channels), Y = 4.0 (the 128 height), Z = 4.0 (the 128 width receding diagonal); the visible true rectangle is the left face (Y×Z projected) and the slab extends toward the viewer along X. Input box: X = 0.75 (3 channels), Y = 4.0, Z = 4.0, centered at (-5.2, 0, 0), subdivided into 3 equal sheets by 2 interior lines on the top and front faces. Output box: X = depth(K), Y = 4.0, Z = 4.0, centered at (+5.2, 0, 0), subdivided into visible sheets with two '...' gaps. Depth mapping (matches the source's compressed look): depth_world(K) = 0.75 + 1.15·log(K/3)/log(32), so K=3 → 0.75 and K=96 → 1.90. Draw at most ~14 sheet lines regardless of K; insert three-dot ellipses (upper receding face and front face) where sheets are omitted. FILTER BOX: rounded rectangle, width 4.4, height 1.9, corner radius 0.35, stroke weight ~0.07, white fill, centered at (0,0,0) facing the camera; text 'Filter Bank with' / '3x3 filters' in a serif face. ARROWS: hollow block arrows (5-sided outline, white fill, black stroke ~0.05), length 1.5, shaft height 0.55, head height 1.1, centered at (-2.7, -0.15, 0) and (+2.7, -0.15, 0), both pointing +X. LABELS: '3' at (-5.2, -2.7, 0) under input; '96' (or current K) at (+5.2, -2.7, 0) under output; '128' at input left vertical edge (-6.6, 0, 0) and along input upper receding edge (-5.0, +2.5, 1.0); '128' at output right vertical edge (+7.6, +0.6, 0) and along output lower receding edge (+7.0, -2.0, -0.5). Headers: 'x_in ∈ R^{3×128×128}' at (-5.2, 3.6, 0), 'x_out ∈ R^{96×128×128}' at (+5.2, 3.6, 0) with the 96 updating with the slider. PALETTE: pure line-art — white fills #FFFFFF, black strokes #000000, black text; highlight color for the selected filter and its linked channels warm orange #E8710A at ~35% fill opacity; secondary spatial-view highlight steel blue #2C6FBB at ~25% fill on the 128×128 faces. Selected-filter overlay: 3×3×3 grid of small cubes, cube side 0.2 with 0.04 gaps, drawn just in front of / above the filter box at (0, 1.6, 0.6), three slices separated along the receding axis, each slice color-matched to the input channel sheet it multiplies.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
