]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.62
}

NOTES: Build slabs as a single BoxGeometry per tensor plus separate thin plane strips for the sheet divider lines; render all edges with LineSegments (EdgesGeometry) at constant screen-space width using a fat-line material so the figure keeps its pen-and-ink look. Use an OrthographicCamera with a shear applied to the projection matrix (or simply place the camera so the left face projects as a true rectangle) for the oblique pose. Labels and headers as CSS2DRenderer/HTML overlays so LaTeX-style math (x_in ∈ R^{3×128×128}) renders crisply with KaTeX and can update live with the slider. Hollow block arrows: ExtrudeGeometry of a 7-point arrow Shape with a white front face and black outline drawn from the shape's path. Rounded rect box: RoundedBoxGeometry or a Shape with quadratic corners, flat-facing camera. When filterCount changes, rebuild only the output slab's divider lines and rescale the box along X (animate over ~250 ms) — keep Y and Z fixed so the preserved 128×128 face is visually obvious. Ellipsis dots are small sphere/circle sprites always billboarded to the camera. Click picking via Raycaster against invisible per-filter proxy planes tiled across the filter box face; on select, set emissive/overlay material on the corresponding input sheets and the one output sheet, and fade all other geometry to 25% opacity. Orbit limited to ±25° azimuth and ±20° elevation around the oblique pose so the figure never loses its diagram-like read.

Follow the interaction plan above.

